From: MacSW maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] comdlg32: Use the macOS file panels when MACSW_NATIVE_FILE_DIALOGS=1.

With MACSW_NATIVE_FILE_DIALOGS=1, IFileDialog::Show (open, save, pick
folders) and GetOpenFileName/GetSaveFileName (A and W) show NSOpenPanel or
NSSavePanel through winemac.drv's macsw_file_dialog export (patch 0023).
Unset or any other value but "all" keeps the Wine dialogs; so does any
failure (no winemac.drv, no export, the panel did not start or did not
report "started" within 10 s).

- macdlg.c: the gate, JSON request writer, unix<->DOS path conversion
  (wine_get_unix_file_name/wine_get_dos_file_name) and the modal loop: the
  top-level owner is disabled like DialogBox does, the thread pumps its
  messages with MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx while it polls the panel,
  WM_QUIT is re-posted, and a destroyed owner cancels the panel. Afterwards
  activation goes back to the owner if comdlg32 disabled it, otherwise to
  the window that was active before, and the focus goes back too. A result
  whose name Windows cannot spell ("q?x.txt") becomes the 8.3 name Wine
  lists for the file.
- itemdlg.c: the dialog state stays where the Wine dialog keeps it (folder,
  selection, file type, the hidden custom control windows, results), so all
  getters keep working. OnFolderChanging/OnFolderChange/OnTypeChange fire
  at start like the Wine dialog; folder, selection and Format changes fire
  them again; accessory controls fire OnCheckButtonToggled, OnItemSelected
  and OnButtonClicked, and changes the application makes in its handlers are
  sent back to the panel, as are SetFileTypeIndex, SetFolder and
  SetFileName calls made at other times. Results become IShellItems; OnOverwrite is called
  but the panel's own overwrite prompt is the only prompt (unless comdlg32
  had to add the extension itself); OnFileOk returning S_FALSE shows the panel
  again where the user was. IFileDialog::Close ends the panel; Show() on a
  dialog whose panel is up returns E_UNEXPECTED.
  The Wine dialog's window exists during a native Show(), hidden: made
  from the same template (without WS_VISIBLE and without the shell browser)
  before the first event and destroyed after OnFileOk, so
  IOleWindow::GetWindow returns it from the first event on, and programs
  that subclass it or put their own windows into it work as with the Wine
  dialog, unseen. SOLIDWORKS's sldfileopen.dll builds its open options pane
  in it and does not check GetWindow's result: with no window, File > Open
  crashed (GetDlgItem on a NULL CWnd). Requests to show or activate it are
  filtered (WM_WINDOWPOSCHANGING, WM_STYLECHANGING, and a check in the panel
  loop); it never gets the focus and is never ended with EndDialog: IDCANCEL
  sent to it, and Close(), end the panel. A program that asks for the window
  in the events fired while the dialog opens gets the visible Wine dialog
  instead (its handlers run again there), unless
  MACSW_NATIVE_FILE_DIALOGS=all. The hidden window then stays, inert, until
  Show() returns, because the program can keep state in it (SOLIDWORKS does
  not build its pane again); so does a fallback after a panel that did not
  start.
- filedlg.c: OPENFILENAME dialogs with a hook or a custom template
  (OFN_ENABLEHOOK with lpfnHook, OFN_ENABLETEMPLATE(HANDLE)) keep the Wine
  dialog, because CDN_* notifications, CDM_* messages and child templates
  need a real dialog window. So do open dialogs with OFN_CREATEPROMPT (and
  IFileOpenDialog with FOS_CREATEPROMPT): they must accept names of files
  that do not exist yet, which NSOpenPanel cannot. Otherwise the result is
  filled like
  FILEDLG95_OnOpen: lpstrFile (double-NUL list for OFN_ALLOWMULTISELECT,
  with the items of the first item's folder only),
  nFileOffset, nFileExtension, nFilterIndex, lpstrFileTitle, the default
  extension (only with lpstrDefExt, only when the name has none),
  OFN_EXTENSIONDIFFERENT, OFN_READONLY ("Open as read-only" box unless
  OFN_HIDEREADONLY), FNERR_BUFFERTOOSMALL, the MRU entry and the current
  directory (unless OFN_NOCHANGEDIR).

